[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say something else.
Every report in it is about a booking on the wrong day, so it was merged by
[subject](/kb/triage/dedupe). Read by what the date did and where it came from, the reports fall
into separate problems. Each needs its own fix, and the fix queued for #4471 closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Fixing the parser leaves the booking-v3 form hint and the one-day moves open.
Each problem now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The counts are built from the ticket export at render time: duplicate rows from overlapping
export pages are counted once, each report is placed by its tenant (locale and booking form,
`#lib/kb/causes`), and the reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) are taken
out of `parser` (`#lib/kb/problems`).

## The problems

### `parser`: the shared parser reads month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so a
date whose day is 12 or lower comes out with day and month swapped: "Our 7 April reminder went
out on 4 July" (R-41200). This is the fix #4471 already has queued. It covers reports from the
app and email channels for every tenant, and from the form for tenants on booking-v2.

### `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells day-first tenants to type month first

Tenants on booking-v3 show `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. Their people type what the hint
says, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The result looks the same as `parser`
(a swapped day and month), but the parser is not involved: the fix is the hint (and the field's
input order) following the tenant's locale. Only reports through the form at booking-v3 tenants
land here. [CauseTable](/kb/incidents/4471) already shows this group as "not this ticket — no
ticket of its own".

### `one-day`: the date moves by one day

These reports have no swap: "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May" (R-41202),
"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting" (R-41344), "Every
booking in June is one day off" (R-41209). A day that moves by one looks like a time-zone or
daylight-saving problem in how invites are sent, not a parsing one. The desk had read these
apart (`oneDayMove`), but the #4471 table still counts them under `parser`, so a parser fix
would have been expected to close them.

## Intake tags are not the cause

Intake tags each report from the reporter's browser. The tenant decides the cause, not the
browser: the notes under the table count how many reports intake tagged as the other cause. Do
not triage these problems by tag.

## The table on #4471, for comparison

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What changes

- Each problem is tracked in its own ticket (linked in the table), with its own count. The fix
  queued for #4471 belongs to `parser`; `hint` and `one-day` need fixes of their own.
- New reports about a wrong date: read the body. A swapped day and month through a booking-v3
  form is `hint`; a date one day off is `one-day`; the rest is `parser`.
- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) accepted that reports with different
  subjects may end up apart. #4471 shows the other side: reports with the same subjects and
  different bugs end up together.

